data.content:
Since `TimestampFormatter` is static and `TimeProvider` comes from DI, the cleanest approach is to **make the class non-static** so it can receive `TimeProvider` via constructor injection:

```csharp
namespace Utils;

public class TimestampFormatter
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public TimestampFormatter(TimeProvider timeProvider)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider;
    }

    public string Now() => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow().ToString("O");
}
```

Then register it in DI:
```csharp
services.AddSingleton<TimestampFormatter>();
```

**Why not keep it static?** Static classes can't take constructor-injected dependencies. The alternatives (service locator, static setter) are anti-patterns that hurt testability — the very reason to use `TimeProvider` in the first place.

